@tests

// What this program refuses, and what it does not, asserted in the program rather than beside it.
//
// The header says the file is scaffolding: every build but `sysl test` drops all of it, and nothing
// outside a test may name any of it. `filled_ring` below is why that is worth writing rather than
// leaving to the pruner — it is an ordinary function, and nothing about the declaration says it
// exists only for the tests under it.
//
// The run in `main.sysl` can only assert what the ring *permits*: a violated `require` traps and
// takes the process with it, so a check that broke one would end the run instead of reporting a
// failure. `@test(should_trap)` is the form that can — each one runs in a process of its own and
// passes by **not** coming back, so the contract that ends it is the thing being measured.
//
// **Every refusal is written next to the call that is not refused.** A `should_trap` test passes for
// any failure at all, including one in its own setup, so on its own it cannot tell "the contract
// fired" from "nothing worked". The pair is what makes it a measurement: one call over the line, one
// call up to it, and the difference between them is the contract.

// A ring filled to capacity, built the only way there is -- the operations themselves, so no fixture
// can construct a state the type system would not have allowed.
//
// The `variant` is the one clause on this page that is about the *loop* rather than about the ring.
// What it says is that each round leaves fewer free slots than the last, which is the whole reason
// this terminates: nothing in `is_full` promises that `push` makes progress towards it. A run that
// stopped making progress would trap here rather than spin, and the measure is what a prover reads
// to discharge termination.
filled_ring() -> Ring
    var r = fresh_ring()

    while !r.is_full()
        variant capacity - usize(r.count)
        r.push(1)

    r
end filled_ring

// -- what the bounds refuse --------------------------------------------------------------------

@test(should_trap)
a_slot_past_the_last_is_not_a_slot() =
    print(int(Slot(8u8)))

@test(should_trap)
a_count_past_capacity_is_not_a_count() =
    print(int(Count(9u8)))

// The asymmetry the two declarations were written for: a full ring has `capacity` elements, so
// `Count` reaches it, and there is no slot with that number, so `Slot` does not. One value, legal as
// one type and not as the other, is the whole of what `new` buys here.
@test
capacity_itself_is_a_count_and_not_a_slot() =
    assert_eq(int(Count(8u8)), 8, "a full ring's count")
    assert(!Slot::Valid(8u8), "and no such slot")

// -- what the contracts refuse, and what they let through -------------------------------------

@test(should_trap)
pushing_past_a_full_ring_traps() =
    var r = filled_ring()

    r.push(99)

@test
but_filling_it_exactly_is_not_refused() =
    var r = filled_ring()

    assert_eq(r.len(), int(capacity), "a whole capacity goes in")

@test(should_trap)
popping_an_empty_ring_traps() =
    var r = fresh_ring()

    print(r.pop())

@test
but_popping_one_that_holds_something_is_not() =
    var r = fresh_ring()

    r.push(7)

    assert_eq(r.pop(), 7, "what went in comes out")
    assert(r.is_empty(), "and it is gone")

// `at`'s precondition is about the elements that are *there*, not about the buffer -- which is the
// distinction a ring buffer exists to keep, since every slot is a real slot whether or not anything
// has been put in it. Slot 2 exists; element 2 does not.
@test(should_trap)
reading_past_the_last_element_traps_although_the_slot_exists() =
    var r = fresh_ring()

    r.push(1)
    r.push(2)

    print(r.at(2))

@test
but_reading_the_last_one_is_not_refused() =
    var r = fresh_ring()

    r.push(1)
    r.push(2)

    assert_eq(r.at(0), 1, "the oldest")
    assert_eq(r.at(1), 2, "and the newest")

// The other end of the same contract, which a bare `i < len` would let through.
@test(should_trap)
a_negative_index_traps() =
    var r = fresh_ring()

    r.push(1)

    print(r.at(-1))

// The total forms are what the program's own run uses, and they answer where these trap. Pinned
// beside them because the pair is the point: a ring offers both, and which one a caller wants is
// which one it can afford to be wrong about.
@test
the_total_forms_answer_where_the_checked_ones_trap() =
    var empty = fresh_ring()

    assert(!empty.try_pop().is_some(), "an empty ring has nothing to give")

    var full = filled_ring()

    assert(!full.try_push(99), "a full ring takes nothing")

// -- what an invariant refuses, mid-update -----------------------------------------------------

// `Tracked` carries one fact twice -- `tail == (head + count) % capacity` -- so a push that moves the
// end and the length one at a time is seen between them, on a state that was on its way to a
// perfectly good one. This is the trap that found the redundant field.
//
// **Neither order escapes it**, which is what rules out reordering as the fix here and leaves the
// whole-struct write as the only way through. The two tests below are the naive push written both
// ways round; the guide's own `push` is the third.
@test(should_trap)
moving_a_tracked_ring_s_end_before_its_length_traps() =
    var t = fresh_tracked()

    t.tail = wrap(t.tail)
    t.count = Count(u8(usize(t.count) + 1))

@test(should_trap)
and_moving_its_length_before_its_end_traps_too() =
    var t = fresh_tracked()

    t.count = Count(u8(usize(t.count) + 1))
    t.tail = wrap(t.tail)

// The form the program actually uses: the same two fields, one write, so there is no moment between
// them for the clause to be asked about.
@test
while_assigning_the_whole_struct_moves_both_at_once() =
    var t = fresh_tracked()

    t.push(1)
    t.push(2)

    assert_eq(t.len(), 2, "both pushes landed")
    assert_eq(int(t.tail), 2, "and the end moved with them")

// The ring the invariant argued for. With the end computed rather than stored there is no clause
// relating two fields, so the same push is one ordinary write and there is nothing to break.
@test
a_ring_that_computes_its_end_has_no_invariant_to_break() =
    var r = fresh_ring()

    r.push(1)
    r.push(2)

    assert_eq(r.len(), 2, "both pushes landed")
    assert_eq(int(r.tail()), 2, "and the end is where they left it")

// -- two fields where ordering the writes is enough --------------------------------------------

// `Watermark` relates two fields too -- `count <= high` -- and needs none of the above, because
// raising the ceiling before the floor keeps every intermediate state legal. The pair is the point:
// the same clause holds under one order and traps under the other, so "reorder the writes" is a real
// answer here and not a hope.
@test
raising_the_ceiling_before_the_floor_keeps_every_state_legal() =
    var w = Watermark(Count(0), Count(0))

    w.take()
    w.take()

    assert_eq(int(w.count), 2, "the floor")
    assert_eq(int(w.high), 2, "and the ceiling it pushed up")

@test(should_trap)
raising_the_floor_first_does_not() =
    var w = Watermark(Count(0), Count(0))

    w.count = Count(u8(usize(w.count) + 1))

// Giving one back lowers the floor and leaves the ceiling where it was, which is what a watermark
// is for -- and is legal in any order, there being only one field to write.
@test
giving_one_back_leaves_the_ceiling_where_it_was() =
    var w = Watermark(Count(0), Count(0))

    w.take()
    w.give()

    assert_eq(int(w.count), 0, "the floor came back down")
    assert_eq(int(w.high), 1, "and the ceiling remembers")
